with d as (select value r from jsonb_array_elements($q$[{"notes":["Keeps well in the fridge for 3 days, or freezes beautifully in portions for up to a month, perfect for reheating before a shift.","If you can't find fenugreek seeds, just use a touch more cumin instead.","Serve with rice, or tear up some bread to dip in.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."],"id":"83900224-8f1a-4dde-bd51-2f990474f097"},{"diets":["no-dairy"],"notes":["Nihari is best made a day ahead: cool completely, refrigerate overnight, and reheat gently before serving. The flavour deepens and the fat layer keeps the meat moist.","For a thicker gravy, mix cornflour with a little water and stir in during the last 15 minutes of cooking.","If you prefer lamb, use lamb shoulder instead of beef and reduce cooking time to 90 minutes.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."],"id":"ba7be9c4-6feb-40fd-9229-8194fd995b00"},{"notes":["If you can't find fresh okra, frozen okra works fine, add it straight to the curry without thawing, though it'll break down a bit more.","Japanese curry paste comes in tubes or blocks from Asian grocers and many supermarkets now. If you can't find it, use 2 tbsp curry powder mixed with 1 tbsp tomato paste and a little water to make a paste.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"b038e224-a5ea-4c9e-b2e3-68c3f22fe1a2"},{"diets":["pescatarian","no-gluten","no-dairy"],"notes":["The sauce is crucial to the QQ texture, it creates that custard centre while the high heat makes the edges crisp. Use tapioca starch if you can find it, it gives better texture than cornflour.","Have all ingredients ready before you start cooking; this is a fast dish once the pan is hot.","Serve immediately. The magic is eating it right away when the edges are still crackling.","This uses raw or lightly cooked egg. Use fresh, clean, uncracked eggs (pasteurised if you can get them), keep it cold, and skip it for anyone pregnant, very young, elderly or with low immunity.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a non-stick or cast iron pan over high heat until very hot, about 2 minutes. The pan should be smoking slightly."},{"text":"While the pan heats, whisk the eggs in a bowl until well beaten. In another bowl, mix the tapioca starch with the water."},{"text":"Pour the oil into the hot pan, tilting to coat evenly. Working quickly, pour in the beaten eggs, letting them spread thin across the pan. Immediately scatter the oysters and spring onion over the egg."},{"text":"Let the eggs set for 1 minute without stirring, until the bottom is golden and crispy. Pour the sauce mixture around and over the eggs."},{"text":"Using a spatula, gently push the omelette toward the centre as the sauce cooks, letting uncooked egg run to the edges, about 2 minutes. You want the edges lacy and crispy while the centre stays creamy."},{"text":"Once the eggs are mostly set but still slightly custard-like in the centre, fold the omelette in half (or leave it open) and slide onto a plate. Drizzle with a little extra sauce and serve immediately while the edges are still QQ."}],"id":"08d0d185-ad76-4218-9b7f-7237242d2397"},{"notes":["Make the brandade the day before if you like; keep it covered in the fridge and bring to room temperature before serving.","The peppers can sit stuffed for a few hours. This is actually better, the flavours marry.","Quality salt cod is essential. Buy from a Spanish deli if you can; ask the owner where they get it."],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the salt cod in cold water for 36 hours, changing the water every 12 hours. Rinse well, then place in a pot of cold water and bring just to a gentle simmer. Cook until the flesh flakes easily, about 15 minutes. Drain and let cool slightly, then flake finely, removing any remaining bones or skin."},{"text":"Meanwhile, peel the potatoes, cut into chunks, and cook in salted boiling water until very tender, about 15 minutes. Drain well and return to the pot."},{"text":"Warm the olive oil gently in a small pan over low heat. Add the sliced garlic and cook slowly, stirring often, until it just begins to turn golden and sweet, about 5 minutes, do not let it brown. Pour the oil and garlic into the potatoes."},{"text":"Mash the potatoes with the garlic oil until completely smooth. Add the flaked bacalao and fold in gently, being careful not to break the fish into a paste. Season carefully with salt and white pepper, remember the salt cod has residual salt."},{"text":"If using tinned piquillo peppers, drain them well on paper towels. Carefully open each pepper and spoon the bacalao brandade into it, filling gently without tearing the pepper."},{"text":"Arrange the stuffed peppers on a serving plate. Serve at room temperature, drizzled lightly with a little extra virgin olive oil and a sprinkle of fresh parsley if you like."}],"id":"506d5be8-d54a-4d96-bf78-de6df0e8b9c7"},{"steps":[{"text":"Fill a large steamer or a pot with a steamer basket with water and bring to the boil until it is bubbling and steam is rising steadily. Pat the fish dry inside and out."},{"text":"Place the fish on a piece of baking paper on the steamer rack. If using a steamer basket, brush the basket lightly with oil first. Steam over boiling water until the fish is just cooked through and the flesh flakes easily, about 12–15 minutes depending on size."},{"text":"While the fish steams, whisk together the fish sauce, lime juice and palm sugar in a small bowl until the sugar dissolves."},{"text":"Transfer the steamed fish to a warm serving plate. Pour the dressing over the hot fish."},{"text":"Top with the fried shallots and fresh chilli slices. Scatter with coriander and serve at once."}],"id":"a14646a3-fa65-4aae-9ef2-5c7db44034c8"},{"notes":["Make the dough and filling the morning before and chill separately. Shape the dumplings while they're cold.","Boiled dumplings can be frozen uncooked on a tray, then transferred to a freezer bag, boil from frozen, adding a couple of minutes to the cooking time.","Cook mince and sausages until they reach 75°C in the centre, with no pink left.","Food safety: Cook mince, sausages, meatballs and liver right through to 75°C, with no pink in the centre."],"steps":[{"text":"Make the dough: mix plain flour with salt in a bowl. Pour in boiling water, stirring with chopsticks until it comes together. Once cool enough to handle, knead on an oiled surface for 10 minutes until smooth. Cover and rest for 30 minutes."},{"text":"Make the filling: combine pork mince, garlic chives, soy sauce, sesame oil, ginger and water in a bowl. Mix gently with chopsticks, don't overwork it or the filling gets tough. Chill until ready to use."},{"text":"Divide the dough into 16 pieces. Roll each into a ball, then flatten with your palm. Using a rolling pin or your fingers, stretch each into a 10 cm circle, keeping the edges thinner than the centre."},{"text":"Place 1 tbsp filling in the centre of each circle. Fold in half to make a half-moon, pinching the edges to seal. Then bring the two pointed ends together and press to seal, forming a purse shape."},{"text":"Bring a large pot of salted water to a boil. Working in batches, gently drop dumplings in and stir so they don't stick. Once they float, simmer for 2–3 minutes more. Remove with a slotted spoon and drain well."},{"text":"Heat 2 tbsp oil in a large frying pan over high heat. Arrange dumplings flat-side down and fry without moving for 3–4 minutes until golden and crisp. Pour in 120 ml water, cover, and steam for 2 minutes. Uncover, let the water evaporate, and fry for another minute until the base is crispy again."},{"text":"Serve hot with soy sauce or chilli oil for dipping."}],"id":"86c19ac6-f9bd-41fb-86b9-71c7135bcd64"},{"notes":["The quenelle mixture can be made up to 4 hours ahead and kept chilled.","If you can't find Madeira, use a good dry sherry instead.","Don't rush the butter-whisking at the end, add it slowly off the heat to keep the sauce silky.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone. Cook mince, sausages, meatballs and liver right through to 75°C, with no pink in the centre."],"steps":[{"text":"Process the chicken liver, softened butter, cream, egg yolks, shallot, salt, pepper and nutmeg until smooth. Chill for at least 20 minutes."},{"text":"Heat the stock in a wide shallow pan to a bare simmer, it should barely bubble. Bring the Madeira to a simmer in a separate small saucepan and add the tomato paste, stirring until it dissolves."},{"text":"Using two warm, wet soup spoons (dip them in hot water and dry well), shape the liver mixture into rough ovals and gently slide them into the simmering stock. Work in batches of 4 or 5. Poach for 4 to 5 minutes, they should rise and feel just firm."},{"text":"Remove the poached quenelles carefully with a slotted spoon to a warm plate. Strain the poaching liquid into a saucepan (discard the solids if any)."},{"text":"Add the Madeira mixture to the poaching liquid and simmer for 3 minutes. Whisk in the cornflour mixture and simmer gently, stirring, until the sauce thickens slightly, about 2 minutes."},{"text":"Remove from heat. Whisk in the cold butter cubes one or two at a time until the sauce is glossy and silky. Taste and adjust the seasoning."},{"text":"Gently reheat the quenelles in the sauce for 1 minute. Divide among warm bowls, spoon the sauce over and finish with a pinch of fleur de sel."}],"id":"744649b0-9fcf-4890-b5f7-f14a1628816e"},{"notes":["Make ahead: sambar keeps well in the fridge for up to 3 days and tastes even better the next day as the flavours deepen. Reheat gently on the stove.","Vegetable swap: use whatever you have, pumpkin, beans, carrots and drumstick are all traditional. Avoid watery vegetables like tomato.","If you have a pressure cooker, this halves the time: toast the spices, cook the base (steps 2–3), then pressure cook for 5 minutes once steaming."],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the cumin seeds, coriander seeds, black pepper and dried chillies in a dry pan over medium heat for 1 minute, shaking the pan, until fragrant. Set aside to cool, then grind to a powder with a mortar and pestle. Stir in the chilli powder."},{"text":"Heat the oil in a large heavy-based pot over medium heat. Add the onion and garlic, and cook, stirring, until the onion is soft and golden, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"Add the asafoetida and toasted spice powder, and stir for 30 seconds until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the lentils, vegetables, tamarind paste, water and salt. Stir well, then bring to a simmer. Cook uncovered for 20 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the lentils have broken down and the vegetables are very tender."},{"text":"Stir in the torn curry leaves and cook for another 2 minutes. Taste and adjust the salt and tamarind to your liking, the stew should be tangy and well-seasoned. Serve hot with steamed rice or dosa."}],"id":"0d6635a3-754e-4b0c-bb67-1cfea7ec947f"},{"diets":["pescatarian","no-gluten","no-dairy"],"notes":["Barramundi, snapper and bream all work beautifully. Avoid oily fish like mackerel here, the flavour's too strong for this delicate broth.","If green papaya is hard to find, use a green mango or even just leave it out. The soup is just as good with more greens instead.","Make the stock the day before if you want the broth to sit overnight in the fridge. Reheat gently before serving.","Food safety: Cook fish until it turns opaque and flakes easily, about 63°C in the thickest part."],"id":"d64aa991-1746-478d-84f6-9930e9ae1fa0"},{"notes":["Make the stock infusion up to an hour ahead; the flavours only get better. Add the fish just before serving.","If you can't find fresh Thai basil, use regular basil, but add it at the last second so it doesn't wilt into the broth.","Barramundi is perfect here because it's sweet and holds its shape, but snapper, bream or any firm white fish works.","Food safety: Cook fish until it turns opaque and flakes easily, about 63°C in the thickest part."],"id":"47aa008a-6a3d-4f23-8ae4-669f18ff44f2"}]$q$::jsonb)), up as (update public.published_recipes p set recipe = p.recipe || (d.r - 'id' - 'notes') || case when d.r ? 'notes' then jsonb_build_object('notes', coalesce((select jsonb_agg(n) from jsonb_array_elements(d.r->'notes') n where btrim(n#>>'{}') <> 'Food safety:'),'[]'::jsonb)) else '{}'::jsonb end, diets = case when d.r ? 'diets' then array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(d.r->'diets')) else p.diets end from d where p.id=(d.r->>'id')::uuid returning p.id) select (select count(*) from d) expected, (select count(*) from up) updated;